<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osorkon I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 887 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osorkon I was the second king of the Twenty-second Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a>, reigning for about 35 years in the late 10th and early 9th centuries BCE. He made lavish gifts to temples across Egypt, recorded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a>. A statue of him inscribed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elibaal">Elibaal</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, shows the continuing ties between Egypt and the Phoenician cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osorkon-i.jpg" alt="Osorkon I" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 3.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Pharaoh_Osorkon_I-AO_9502-P5280216-gradient.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sekhemkheperre Osorkon I was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the 22nd Dynasty. According to the stela of Pasenhor he was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a> and his chief consort Karomama A. He married Maatkare B, a king's daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-ii">Psusennes II</a> of the 21st Dynasty, and their son Shoshenq-Meryamun served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> during his reign. His throne name, Sekhemkheperre, means Powerful are the Manifestations of Re. His territory included much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a> is thought to have reigned 21 regnal years, and Osorkon I succeeded him. His reign is known for many temple building projects and was a long and prosperous period. The Donation Stela of Year 6, also called the Met Stela, shows him offering a field hieroglyph to the gods Re-Harakhty and Nebet-Hetepet, and records a donation of land in the Heliopolis region. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> level record at the Karnak Quay dates to Year 12. At Shibin El Qanater a bronze and gold statuette bears his names with the gods Re-Horakhty and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> a bust of Osorkon is one of five Byblian royal inscriptions.</p><p>His highest date, Year 33, comes from the bandage of the mummy of Nakhtefmut, which held a menat-tab necklace inscribed with his names. Bandages on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> mummy of Khonsmaakheru give Years 11, 12 and 23. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> gives him 15 years, which Kenneth Kitchen thinks most likely an error for 35, the length supported by a second Heb Sed bandage. He died at an advanced age and was succeeded by Takelot I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His tomb has never been found. His reign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was peaceful and uneventful, though his son Takelot I and grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-ii">Osorkon II</a> later had difficulty controlling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> because of a rival king, Harsiese A.</p><p>Takelot I was a son of Osorkon I by Queen Tashedkhons, a secondary wife. It is possible that Heqakheperre Shoshenq II reigned briefly between them. Kitchen views Shoshenq II as the High Priest Shoshenq-Meryamun, a short-lived coregent who died before his father. Jürgen von Beckerath holds that he was an independent king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> who ruled for about two years, citing his full royal titulary and an intact tomb with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> falcon-headed coffin and a gold face mask, none of which mentions Osorkon I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osorkon I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…end of Egyptian rule in the Levant in the twelfth century BCE it survived the upheavals that destroyed cities such as Ugarit, and in the tenth century its kings were still prosperous enough to dedicate inscribed statues of the pharaohs Shoshenq I and <b>Osorkon I</b> to their goddess.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Osorkon I</b> and Osorkon II endowed temples lavishly, and Osorkon II rebuilt at Bubastis.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-second-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-second Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
